// Reactivity S5 (hardening) — a `computed` can derive from several signals. `total` reads both `a` and
// `b`; it recomputes (echoing "compute") only when one of them has actually changed, and returns its
// memo otherwise. The witness: two reads with no change between them compute once; changing `a` then
// reading recomputes; changing `b` then reading recomputes; so the derivation tracks every source but
// still memoizes across unrelated reads.
// expect: stdout "compute"
// expect: stdout "total=3"
// expect: stdout "total=3"
// expect: stdout "compute"
// expect: stdout "total=12"
// expect: stdout "compute"
// expect: stdout "total=30"
// expect: exit 0
use std.reactive.{signal, computed}
a = signal(1)
b = signal(2)
total = computed(fn() { echo "compute"; return a.get() + b.get() })
echo "total=${total.get()}"
echo "total=${total.get()}"
a.set(10)
echo "total=${total.get()}"
b.set(20)
echo "total=${total.get()}"
